\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Tipping direction and press-point selection. (\textbf  {left}) Support-footprint geometry, viewed from above, for representative pivot cases. For a straight-edge pivot, the desired tipping axis $\hat  {\mathbf  e}$ is aligned with the edge and the pull direction $\hat  {\mathbf  d}$ is chosen approximately normal to it. For a vertex or locally curved support boundary, contact reduces to a single expected pivot point and $\hat  {\mathbf  e}$ is taken as the local tangent to the support boundary. (\textbf  {right}) Press-point selection on a segmented object point cloud $\mathcal  {P}$, shown for the pull direction $\hat  {\mathbf  d} = \hat  {\mathbf  d}_{\mathrm  {pull}}$. Candidate points are first screened by the surface-normal condition in Eq.~\eqref  {eq:press_feasible_set} to reject contacts likely to separate under the commanded pull. For each remaining point, the lever arm $\mathbf  r_i$ and resulting moment $\boldsymbol  {\tau }_i = \mathbf  r_i \times \hat  {\mathbf  d}$ are evaluated. The selected point $\mathbf  p^*$ maximizes the signed moment about the desired tipping axis according to Eq.~\eqref  {eq:press_point_selection}. Forward tipping reuses the same construction at $\hat  {\mathbf  d}_{\mathrm  {push}}$ (Section~\ref {sec:forward_tip_selection}).}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:contact_selection}{{2}{6}{Tipping direction and press-point selection. (\textbf {left}) Support-footprint geometry, viewed from above, for representative pivot cases. For a straight-edge pivot, the desired tipping axis $\hat {\mathbf e}$ is aligned with the edge and the pull direction $\hat {\mathbf d}$ is chosen approximately normal to it. For a vertex or locally curved support boundary, contact reduces to a single expected pivot point and $\hat {\mathbf e}$ is taken as the local tangent to the support boundary. (\textbf {right}) Press-point selection on a segmented object point cloud $\mathcal {P}$, shown for the pull direction $\hat {\mathbf d} = \hat {\mathbf d}_{\mathrm {pull}}$. Candidate points are first screened by the surface-normal condition in Eq.~\eqref {eq:press_feasible_set} to reject contacts likely to separate under the commanded pull. For each remaining point, the lever arm $\mathbf r_i$ and resulting moment $\boldsymbol {\tau }_i = \mathbf r_i \times \hat {\mathbf d}$ are evaluated. The selected point $\mathbf p^*$ maximizes the signed moment about the desired tipping axis according to Eq.~\eqref {eq:press_point_selection}. Forward tipping reuses the same construction at $\hat {\mathbf d}_{\mathrm {push}}$ (Section~\ref {sec:forward_tip_selection})}{figure.2}{}}
